.TH DAG\-REPLAY 1 "2026-11-13" "KariDNS" "KariDNS Manual"
.\" Generated from docs/dag_replay.md by man/md2man.pl (make man): edit the Markdown manual, not this page.
.SH NAME
dag\-replay \- High-Performance DNS Traffic Replay and Differential Testing
Engine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.RS 2
.nf
dag \-\-replay <traffic_file> \-\-server1 <host[:port]> [\-\-server2 <host[:port]>]
    [\-\-compare\-recorded] [\-\-diff] [\-\-ignore\-ttl] [\-\-output\-diff <file>]
    [\-\-rate <qps>] [\-\-workers <N>] [\-\-timeout\-ms <ms>]
    [\-\-stop\-after <N>] [\-\-max\-queries <N>]
    [\-\-transport <udp|tcp|tls|doh>] [\-\-server1\-transport <transport>] [\-\-server2\-transport <transport>]
    [\-\-output <text|json>] [+dnssec | +do] [+nodnssec | +nodo]
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBdag \-\-replay\fR is an asynchronous, multi-threaded traffic replay and
differential testing utility built into the \fBdag(1)\fR diagnostic suite.
.PP
It reads DNS queries from live traffic capture files (\fBPCAP\fR, \fBdnstap /
Frame Streams\fR) or plain text query lists, transmits them concurrently across
one or two target DNS nameservers, and evaluates response consistency.
.PP
When dual servers are specified (\fB\-\-server1\fR and \fB\-\-server2\fR), or
when single server with recorded responses is specified
(\fB\-\-compare\-recorded\fR), \fBdag \-\-replay\fR performs \fBsemantic,
order-independent differential comparison\fR of the responses across every DNS
section (Header, Question, Answer, Authority, and Additional). It categorizes
differences into granular bitmask flags, detects subtle protocol inconsistencies
(such as glue record omissions, DNSSEC RRSIG/NSEC differences, and CNAME chain
discrepancies), and outputs structured statistics in human-readable or JSON
format.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Server Target Specifications
.TP 8
\fB\-\-server1 <host[:port]>\fR
Address and optional port of the primary target nameserver. Without a port, the
default of the server's transport is used: \fB53\fR (udp/tcp), \fB853\fR (tls),
\fB443\fR (doh). Accepted forms: \fBhost\fR, \fBhost:port\fR, \fBhost#port\fR,
\fB[IPv6]\fR, \fB[IPv6]:port\fR, \fB[IPv6]#port\fR and \fBIPv6#port\fR
(e.g. \fB192.0.2.1:5353\fR, \fB[2001:db8::1]:5353\fR, \fB2001:db8::1#5353\fR). A
bare IPv6 address without brackets or \fB#\fR is taken as an address without a
port. \fB%\fR is not accepted as a port separator because it is the IPv6 zone ID
delimiter (RFC 6874). An invalid value or a port outside 1-65535 is a usage
error (exit 1). IPv6 targets are shown as \fB[addr]:port\fR in the reports.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-server2 <host[:port]>\fR
Address and optional port of the secondary comparison nameserver (default port
as for \fB\-\-server1\fR). Specifying \fB\-\-server2\fR automatically enables
differential testing mode (\fB\-\-diff\fR). Mutually exclusive with
\fB\-\-compare\-recorded\fR.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-compare\-recorded\fR
Compare live responses from \fB\-\-server1\fR against the original recorded
responses stored within the capture file (\fB.pcap\fR or \fB.dnstap\fR). Does
not require a secondary live server. Mutually exclusive with
\fB\-\-server2\fR. Not supported for text query files (\fBqueries.txt\fR). It is
strongly recommended to specify \fB\-\-ignore\-ttl\fR with this option to avoid
false differences caused by normal cache/TTL aging.
.SS Replay & Engine Control
.TP 8
\fB\-\-replay <traffic_file>\fR
Path to the input traffic source. Automatically detects format:
.RS 8
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPCAP file\fR (magic \fB0xa1b2c3d4\fR or \fB0xd4c3b2a1\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdnstap Frame Streams file\fR (initial escape word \fB0x00000000\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBText query file\fR (line-by-line domain name and type)
.RE
.TP 8
\fB\-\-rate <qps>\fR, \fB\-\-rate\-limit <qps>\fR
Limit the maximum query transmission rate to the specified queries-per-second
(QPS). Replay pacing applies microsecond sleeps between task generation. When
omitted or set to \fB0\fR, queries are dispatched as fast as the workers and
targets permit.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-workers <N>\fR, \fB\-\-concurrency <N>\fR
Number of concurrent worker threads processing the replay queue (default:
\fB1\fR, maximum: \fB64\fR). Each worker thread independently handles network
exchanges and response comparisons.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-max\-queries <N>\fR
Stop replay processing after dispatching \fBN\fR queries.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-timeout\-ms <ms>\fR
Network socket I/O timeout in milliseconds for each query-response exchange
(default: \fB2000\fR ms; \fB0\fR or a negative value means the default).
.SS Differential Testing Options
.TP 8
\fB\-\-diff\fR
Explicitly enable differential response comparison. Automatically activated when
\fB\-\-server2\fR or \fB\-\-compare\-recorded\fR is provided.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-ignore\-ttl\fR
Ignore TTL differences when comparing resource records across servers or against
recorded responses. When enabled, two identical record sets with differing TTL
values (e.g., due to caching state, TTL countdown, or differing zone minimum TTL
configurations) are treated as identical. Strongly recommended when using
\fB\-\-compare\-recorded\fR.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-stop\-after <N>\fR
Early termination threshold. Stops reading the input and discards the queued
queries once \fBN\fR mismatched responses have been found (between
\fB\-\-server1\fR and \fB\-\-server2\fR, or against the recorded
responses). Queries already being processed by the workers are still completed.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-output\-diff <file>\fR
Write one line per mismatched query to \fB<file>\fR. Use \fB\-\fR to stream the
lines to \fBstdout\fR. Without this option, the first mismatches (while at most
10 responses have been compared) are printed to \fBstderr\fR in text output
mode.
.SS Transport Control
.TP 8
\fB\-\-transport <udp|tcp|tls|doh>\fR
Explicitly force the global transport protocol for all replayed queries,
overriding any transport recorded in the input capture file. \fBtls\fR (alias
\fBdot\fR) is DNS over TLS (RFC 7858, ALPN \fBdot\fR) and \fBdoh\fR (alias
\fBhttps\fR) is DNS over HTTPS (RFC 8484, \fBPOST /dns\-query\fR over
HTTP/1.1). Each DoT/DoH query uses its own TLS connection; server certificates
are not verified (as with \fBdag +tls\fR / \fB+https\fR without \fB+tls\-ca\fR),
and SNI is sent when the server is given by name. Any other value is an error
(exit status 1).
.TP 8
\fB\-\-server1\-transport <udp|tcp|tls|doh>\fR
Force the transport protocol specifically for queries sent to
\fB\-\-server1\fR. Allows cross-transport differential testing (e.g., UDP
vs. TCP validation on the same server).
.TP 8
\fB\-\-server2\-transport <udp|tcp|tls|doh>\fR
Force the transport protocol specifically for queries sent to \fB\-\-server2\fR.
.SS Query Options & Output Format
.TP 8
\fB+dnssec\fR, \fB+do\fR
Set the DNSSEC OK (DO) bit (0x8000) in the EDNS0 OPT record for synthetic
queries generated from text query lists.
.TP 8
\fB+nodnssec\fR, \fB+nodo\fR
Clear the DO bit in EDNS0 OPT records for synthetic text queries (default).
.TP 8
\fB\-\-output <text|json>\fR, \fB\-\-output\-format <text|json>\fR
Select report output format. \fBtext\fR (default) prints a formatted console
summary. \fBjson\fR emits a machine-readable JSON document containing latency
histograms, RCODE breakdowns, and differential counters.
.SH INPUT FORMATS & PROTOCOL PRESERVATION
\fBdag \-\-replay\fR supports three distinct traffic input sources:
.SS 1. dnstap Frame Streams (.fstrm / .dnstap)
Standard binary log format specified by Farsight Security and widely implemented
in KariDNS, BIND 9, Unbound, NSD, and Knot DNS.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBHandshake & Frame Handling\fR: Parses the bidirectional Frame Streams
container, safely skipping control frames (\fBSTART\fR, \fBSTOP\fR, \fBREADY\fR,
\fBACCEPT\fR, \fBFINISH\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBProtobuf Deserialization\fR: Decodes Google Protocol Buffers
\fBdnstap.Dnstap\fR wrapper and extracts \fBdnstap.Message\fR content.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBQuery Extraction\fR: Extracts \fBquery_message\fR (field 10) wire
payloads. Verifies \fBQR == 0\fR and \fBQDCOUNT >= 1\fR, automatically ignoring
server response frames (\fBresponse_message\fR, field 14).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTransport Fidelity\fR: Extracts \fBsocket_protocol\fR (field 3: \fB1 =
UDP\fR, \fB2 = TCP\fR). Replayed queries faithfully reproduce the recorded
transport protocol without requiring command-line flags.
.SS 2. PCAP Live Captures (.pcap)
Standard packet capture format generated by \fBtcpdump\fR, \fBwireshark\fR, or
switch port mirrors.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLink Layer Decoding\fR: Supports Ethernet (\fBLINKTYPE_ETHERNET\fR, 1), Linux
cooked capture (\fBLINKTYPE_LINUX_SLL\fR, 113, and \fBLINKTYPE_LINUX_SLL2\fR,
276, as written by \fBtcpdump \-i any\fR on current Linux), Raw IP
(\fBLINKTYPE_RAW\fR, 12 / 101), BSD loopback (\fBLINKTYPE_NULL\fR, 0: 4-byte
address family in the capturing host's byte order) and OpenBSD loopback
(\fBLINKTYPE_LOOP\fR, 108: the same in network byte order). Other link types are
auto-detected as raw IP or Ethernet.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBVLAN Support\fR: Transparently unpacks 802.1Q tagged Ethernet frames
(Ethertype \fB0x8100\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNetwork & Transport Layer\fR: Inspects IPv4 and IPv6 headers. Correctly
handles variable-length IP options and TCP header extensions.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBProtocol Fidelity\fR: Distinguishes between UDP (\fBIPPROTO_UDP\fR, 17) and
TCP (\fBIPPROTO_TCP\fR, 6). For TCP captures, the 2-byte DNS length prefix is
stripped and parsed into wire-format queries. Original transport is retained for
each replayed packet.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBQuery Filtering\fR: Validates that the packet is a query (\fBQR == 0\fR) with
at least one question. Non-DNS or response packets are ignored.
.SS 3. Plain Text Query Lists (.txt)
Convenient human-readable query list format with one entry per line:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
# Domain Name               QTYPE   [Options]
example.com                 A
www.example.com             AAAA
sec.example.com             DNSKEY  +dnssec
mail.example.com            MX      +tcp
corp.example.com            AXFR    +tcp
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fBComment & Empty Line Skipping\fR: Lines beginning with \fB#\fR or \fB;\fR and
blank lines are ignored.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTransport Flags\fR: Adding \fB+tcp\fR or \fB+udp\fR on a line sets the
transport for that individual query.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDNSSEC Flags\fR: Adding \fB+dnssec\fR or \fB+do\fR sets the DO bit in EDNS0
OPT for that query.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPacket Construction\fR: Automatically generates RFC 1035 compliant DNS wire
packets with randomized Transaction IDs and standard RD flags.
.SH TRANSPORT RESOLUTION HIERARCHY
When dispatching queries to target servers, \fBdag \-\-replay\fR determines the
transport protocol using the following strict priority:
.IP 1. 4
\fBPer-Server Transport Option (\fB\-\-server1\-transport\fB /
\fB\-\-server2\-transport\fB)\fR If set on the CLI, this overrides all other
settings for the corresponding server.
.IP 2. 4
\fBGlobal Transport Option (\fB\-\-transport\fB)\fR If explicitly specified on
the CLI (e.g., \fB\-\-transport tcp\fR), forces all replayed queries across both
servers to use the given transport.
.IP 3. 4
\fBCapture Transport (\fBtask.transport\fB)\fR If no CLI transport override is
provided, the engine uses the original transport recorded in the input:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
dnstap: \fBMessage.socket_protocol\fR (\fBUDP\fR or \fBTCP\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
PCAP: L4 IP header protocol (\fBUDP\fR or \fBTCP\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
Text files: Per-line \fB+tcp\fR or \fB+udp\fR directive
.RE
.IP 4. 4
\fBDefault Transport\fR If none of the above apply, defaults to \fB"udp"\fR.
.SH DIFFERENTIAL COMPARISON ENGINE
When comparing responses from \fB\-\-server1\fR and \fB\-\-server2\fR, \fBdag
\-\-replay\fR employs an order-independent RRset comparison algorithm. DNS
servers are permitted by RFC 1035 and RFC 2181 to return resource records within
an RRset in any order (round-robin rotation) and use varying compression pointer
schemes.
.PP
The comparison engine normalizes and evaluates differences across 13 distinct
categories:
.PP
.TS
lb lb lbw(44)
l l lw(44).
\&Flag Name	\&Bitmask	\&Description
_
\&\fB\fBDIFF_RCODE\fB\fR	\&\fB0x0001\fR	T{
Response RCODE mismatch (e.g., \fBNOERROR\fR vs. \fBNXDOMAIN\fR or
\fBSERVFAIL\fR).
T}
\&\fB\fBDIFF_FLAGS\fB\fR	\&\fB0x0002\fR	\&Header flags mismatch (\fBAA\fR or \fBTC\fR).
\&\fB\fBDIFF_ANCOUNT\fB\fR	\&\fB0x0004\fR	\&Record count mismatch in the Answer section.
\&\fB\fBDIFF_NSCOUNT\fB\fR	\&\fB0x0008\fR	T{
Record count mismatch in the Authority section.
T}
\&\fB\fBDIFF_ARCOUNT\fB\fR	\&\fB0x0010\fR	T{
Record count mismatch in the Additional section.
T}
\&\fB\fBDIFF_ANSWER_RRSET\fB\fR	\&\fB0x0020\fR	T{
Semantic mismatch in Answer RRset data (owner name, type, class, or RDATA).
T}
\&\fB\fBDIFF_AUTH_RRSET\fB\fR	\&\fB0x0040\fR	\&Semantic mismatch in Authority RRset data.
\&\fB\fBDIFF_ADD_RRSET\fB\fR	\&\fB0x0080\fR	\&Semantic mismatch in Additional RRset data.
\&\fB\fBDIFF_GLUE_MISSING\fB\fR	\&\fB0x0100\fR	T{
In-bailiwick glue record missing or mismatched in referral responses.
T}
\&\fB\fBDIFF_EDNS\fB\fR	\&\fB0x0200\fR	T{
EDNS0 presence, UDP payload size, extended RCODE, version, DO bit, COOKIE
presence, or option mismatch.
T}
\&\fB\fBDIFF_DNSSEC_RRSIG\fB\fR	\&\fB0x0400\fR	T{
DNSSEC RRSIG signature presence, type covered, or validity difference.
T}
\&\fB\fBDIFF_DNSSEC_NSEC\fB\fR	\&\fB0x0800\fR	T{
DNSSEC NSEC / NSEC3 denial of existence record difference.
T}
\&\fB\fBDIFF_CNAME_CHAIN\fB\fR	\&\fB0x1000\fR	T{
CNAME redirection alias chain length or target mismatch.
T}
.TE
.SH OUTPUT FORMATS
.SS 1. Standard Text Report (Default)
Printed to standard output upon completion. The server 2 and differential blocks
appear only with \fB\-\-server2\fR or \fB\-\-compare\-recorded\fR (with
\fB\-\-compare\-recorded\fR the second block is titled \fBRecorded (from
capture)\fR and shows the capture file instead of an address, without an RTT):
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
================ DNS Replay Summary ================
Total Queries Replayed: 1000
Transport:              udp
Workers:                1
\&
\-\-\- Server 1 (127.0.0.1:53) \-\-\-
  Server 1: 1000 responses
  Sent:     1000
  Received: 1000
  Timeouts: 0
  Avg RTT:  0.42 ms
  RCODEs:   NOERROR=950 NXDOMAIN=50 SERVFAIL=0 REFUSED=0
\&
\-\-\- Server 2 (127.0.0.1:5353) \-\-\-
  Responses: 1000
  Sent/Pairs: 1000
  Received:  1000
  Timeouts:  0
  Avg RTT:   0.38 ms
  RCODEs:    NOERROR=950 NXDOMAIN=50 SERVFAIL=0 REFUSED=0
\&
\-\-\- Differential Comparison \-\-\-
  Compared:           1000
  Identical responses: 998 (99.8%)
  Mismatched responses: 2
  RCODE Mismatches:   0
  Flags Mismatches:   0
  ANCOUNT Mismatches: 1
  RRset Mismatches:   1
  Glue Missing:       0
  EDNS Diffs:         0
  DNSSEC RRSIG Diffs: 1
  DNSSEC NSEC Diffs:  0
  CNAME Chain Diffs:  0
====================================================
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBTransport\fR shows the \fB\-\-transport\fR value (default \fBudp\fR), not the
per-query transport taken from the capture.
.SS 2. JSON Report (\-\-output json)
Structured document suitable for CI/CD assertions and telemetry ingestion. With
\fB\-\-compare\-recorded\fR, the second object is named \fBrecorded\fR and has a
\fBsource\fR (the capture file) instead of \fBtarget\fR; without a second server
or \fB\-\-compare\-recorded\fR, only \fBtotal_queries\fR and \fBserver1\fR are
printed.
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
{
  "total_queries": 1000,
  "server1": {
    "target": "127.0.0.1:53",
    "sent": 1000,
    "received": 1000,
    "timeouts": 0,
    "avg_rtt_ms": 0.42,
    "noerror": 950,
    "nxdomain": 50,
    "servfail": 0
  },
  "server2": {
    "target": "127.0.0.1:5353",
    "sent": 1000,
    "received": 1000,
    "timeouts": 0,
    "avg_rtt_ms": 0.38,
    "noerror": 950,
    "nxdomain": 50,
    "servfail": 0
  },
  "diff": {
    "compared": 1000,
    "identical": 998,
    "identical_queries": 998,
    "mismatched_queries": 2,
    "rcode_mismatches": 0,
    "flags_mismatches": 0,
    "ancount_mismatches": 1,
    "rrset_mismatches": 1,
    "glue_missing_diffs": 0,
    "edns_diffs": 0,
    "dnssec_rrsig_diffs": 1,
    "dnssec_nsec_diffs": 0,
    "cname_chain_diffs": 0
  }
}
.fi
.RE
.SS 3. Difference Stream (\-\-output-diff <file>)
Each mismatch records the query sequence number, the names of the differing
categories, and the hexadecimal bitmask:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
[DIFF #42] Reachability mismatch: Live=OK Recorded/S2=TIMEOUT (flags=0x0001)
[DIFF #108] Diff: [DNSSEC_RRSIG]  (flags=0x0400)
[DIFF #512] Diff: [ANCOUNT] [ANSWER]  (flags=0x0024)
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP 8
\fB0\fR
The replay ran to completion (or was stopped by \fB\-\-max\-queries\fR /
\fB\-\-stop\-after\fR) and, when responses were compared (\fB\-\-server2\fR or
\fB\-\-compare\-recorded\fR), no mismatch was found. A single-server replay
without comparison always exits with 0.
.TP 8
\fB1\fR
Responses were compared and at least one mismatch was found
(\fBmismatched_queries > 0\fR, including one server not answering); or a usage
error (missing \fB\-\-replay\fR / \fB\-\-server1\fR, unknown transport),
\fB\-\-compare\-recorded\fR combined with \fB\-\-server2\fR or with a text query
file, the input file cannot be opened, TLS could not be initialized, or a memory
allocation failure.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS 1. Replay dnstap Traffic to a Target Nameserver
Replay a live dnstap log captured via \fBfstrm_capture\fR or KariDNS against a
local server:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
dag \-\-replay /var/log/dnstap.fstrm \-\-server1 127.0.0.1:53
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fINote: Queries that were originally received over TCP will automatically be
replayed over TCP; queries received over UDP will be replayed over UDP.\fR
.SS 2. Differential Regression Testing Across Implementations
Verify consistency between BIND 9 and KariDNS using a captured PCAP trace:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
dag \-\-replay traffic.pcap \e
    \-\-server1 127.0.0.1:53 \e
    \-\-server2 127.0.0.1:5353 \e
    \-\-diff \-\-ignore\-ttl \e
    \-\-output\-diff /tmp/differences.log
.fi
.RE
.SS 3. High-Concurrency Stress & Benchmarking
Replay a 100,000 query text list at 10,000 QPS across 16 worker threads:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
dag \-\-replay queries.txt \e
    \-\-server1 192.0.2.1:53 \e
    \-\-rate 10000 \e
    \-\-workers 16
.fi
.RE
.SS 4. Continuous Integration Early Abort
In automated CI pipelines, stop at the first response mismatch; the exit status
is 1 when a mismatch was found:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
dag \-\-replay regression_suite.txt \e
    \-\-server1 127.0.0.1:53 \e
    \-\-server2 127.0.0.1:1053 \e
    \-\-diff \-\-stop\-after 1 \e
    \-\-output json
.fi
.RE
.SS 5. Cross-Protocol Validation (UDP vs. TCP)
Verify that a server produces identical answers whether queried over UDP or TCP:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
dag \-\-replay queries.txt \e
    \-\-server1 127.0.0.1:53 \-\-server1\-transport udp \e
    \-\-server2 127.0.0.1:53 \-\-server2\-transport tcp \e
    \-\-diff
.fi
.RE
.PP
Compare a server's DNS over TLS (port 853) and DNS over HTTPS (port 443) front
ends with its plain TCP answers:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
dag \-\-replay queries.txt \e
    \-\-server1 dns.example.net \-\-server1\-transport tls \e
    \-\-server2 dns.example.net \-\-server2\-transport tcp \-\-diff
\&
dag \-\-replay queries.txt \e
    \-\-server1 dns.example.net \-\-server1\-transport doh \e
    \-\-server2 dns.example.net \-\-server2\-transport tcp \-\-diff
.fi
.RE
.SS 6. Single-Server Recorded Response Comparison
Verify live server behavior against baseline responses recorded in a PCAP or
dnstap capture without spinning up a second server:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
dag \-\-replay traffic.dnstap \e
    \-\-server1 127.0.0.1:53 \e
    \-\-compare\-recorded \e
    \-\-ignore\-ttl
.fi
.RE
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
dag \-\-replay traffic.pcap \e
    \-\-server1 127.0.0.1:53 \e
    \-\-compare\-recorded \e
    \-\-ignore\-ttl \e
    \-\-output json
.fi
.RE
